1. e4 { King's Pawn opening e4, one of the most common move for white to start. } 1... g5 { g5 is the basic first move for the Borg defense as reversed to the Grob opening g4. It opens the diagonal for the f8 bishop and can disturb white knight to go to f3. } 2. d4 { The best respons for white against the borg. White conquers the center and attacks the hanging pawn on g5. When black moves his bishop to Bg7 the respons e5 limits his effectiveness. } 2... e6 { This moves opens the diagonal for the black Queen to protect the pawn on g5. It also supports the move d5. Placing the white Bishop on Bc4 has no treat for f7 for example if the white Queen joins the attack on Qf3 of Qh5. The most natural way to proceed for white is to develop his knights (Nc3, Nf3 or Nh3) or bishops (Be3) but in fact the way to go is with the pawns on h4 or even better e4. } (2... h6 { This is also a common move played by Basman mostly but according to Stockfish (37 deep) not the best one. It advantage is that black does for the time being not to keep an eye on g5 anymore in his further development. But this extra move to protect the pawn costs also some tempo compared to e6. Besides that, with h4 white can easily break up thepawns. This move was played although by Carlsen in his matches against GMs' Firouzja, Hansen, Praggnanandhaa & Andrew Tang, Lichess Bullet Titled Arena, June 2019 } 3. h4 { This attack from white on de the pawn structure allows black to go for the Troon gambit by g4. The intention for black is to chase the white Queen. }) (2... d5 3. e5) 3. c4 { The best move. White protects d5. } (3. Nf3 g4 { Chase the Knight seems the best approach. If the white knight goes to Ne5 black can protect the g4 pawn with h5 first and then attack with Bg7. } 4. Ng5 { Looses the knight }) (3. Nc3 d5 4. exd5 exd5 5. Be2 Be6) (3. f4 { Black can choose to exchange or play for a gambit }) (3. e5 h5 4. h4) 3... d6 4. Nc3 h6 5. h4 gxh4 6. Be2 Nc6 7. Nf3 Nf6 8. Rxh4 *


