1. e4 g5 2. Bc4 { Beware of the white Queen on Qh5 or Qf3! So any move with the f-pawn is of course fatal. Best move is h6 in spite of threat of the Scholar's mate. If the Queen comes, reply simple with e6 which is a strong move anyway. } 2... h6 3. h4 (3. d4) (3. Qh5 e6 { Now white has to be careful not to fall into a trap itself. He has to retreat the Queen and not block it by Nf3. Otherwise Nf6 will chase the Queen without escaping a fork by g4. An exchange of Queens by d4 and after Nf6 the bishop get's Bg4, does also not work without losing material by white. } 4. Nf3 (4. d4 Nf6 5. Bxg5 Nxh5 6. Bxd8 Kxd8) 4... Nf6 5. Qh3 g4) (3. Qf3 Nf6) 3... gxh4 (3... g4 { If the white Queen now accepts the Troon gambit, it loses the white bishop by d5. } 4. Qxg4 d5) 4. d4 (4. Rxh4 e6 5. Nf3 d5 6. exd5 exd5 7. Be2 Nc6) 4... d6 *


