1. e4 g5 2. h4 { An exchange will result in an open file for white on h and a developed rook. Black opens the g file. Also castling becomes for both sides more dangerous. The game is more open after the exchange which is less favourable for black who has less development. The isolated h-pawn of black is not very crucial. A white knight on f3 can not be harassed anymore. } 2... g4 { This closes the position for black which makes it safer. If the white Queen accepts, chase her away by d5. Do not capture the pawn but proceed to d4. If white rejects the gambit by Nc3 protecting the pawn on e4 and the square d5, black only can continue the gambit by playing his pawn to c6. } 3. Nc3 c6 { Now again the same situation is there. If black accepts the gambit, by d5 the Queen and the pawn on e5 is attacked. And again do not take the pawn on e4 but proceed to d4. And after the white knight has moved, continue with Nf6 attacking the hanging pawn on e4 locking in white to protect black's king side. To prevent this, white could respond with d3. But with Bg4 the lock in continues. } 4. Qxg4 d5 5. Qg3 d4 6. Nce2 c5 7. Nf4 Qd6 *


