1. e4 g5 2. Qg4 d5 { This approach by white makes the positions almost equal. White can take take the g4 pawn but also retreating to Qg3 is fine. When he takes in fact he traded for his queen pawn for a flank pawn but with hanging center pawn that can be attacked. White's Queen though can also become a victim for black's attacking forces. } 3. Qg3 { This gives black the initiative. He can choose to exchange and open the game, or witjh e6 close it. When he conquers the pawn e4 we can end up in the same position as if white wpuld accepted the exchange right away only white has lost a tempo. So the refusal must implicate that white has other plans than capturing the g5 pawn. For example the unnatural looking move e3, which seems to allow black to exchange his hanging Queen's pawn for that of white, ending up with an extra pawn. } (3. Qxg5 dxe4 { Now white can attack the hanging pawn. The best piece for that is the knight on Nc3. }) 3... dxe4 4. d3 exd3 { Although it looks like black has the advantage, beacuse of the position of white, the changes are slightly better for white beause of it's development. } 5. Bxd3 *


